+## Control-flow lifetime and alias handling
+
+An if-result keeps its branch-local sources alive until its last use. Sources
+for the same return slot belong to one phi family, so mutually exclusive arms
+can share storage despite that lifetime extension. This also covers branch-local
+aliases of an in-place gather loop's allocation; definitions outside the arm
+remain independent and can still prevent reuse.
+
+Under capacity pressure only, the packer can analyze the opposite branch order
+(else before then) once and retry. Disjoint lifetimes in either order establish
+non-interference without changing execution order. For example, a value read
+only in the else arm can share with a fresh replacement in the then arm. Reads
+after the if, same-arm tail reads, loop recurrence, pipeline separation and
+target hazards remain constraints. Layouts that already fit are unchanged.
+This adds a bounded traversal and packing attempt, not a search over branch
+permutations.
+
+Yield fixup immediately retypes pure aliases of an updated inner phi before
+resolving an outer phi. For all planners, when both arms already refer to the
+same physical window, their phi retains that window. This does not introduce
+opportunistic sharing or divergent-arm copies under PTOAS.
+
+During loop-carry retargeting, a pure SSA identity may still carry a stale
+MemRef after an inner phi has moved. The loop initializer independently fixes
+the destination, so `TopDownRetargeter` follows the identity to its producer
+without requiring the old identity window to match first. Producer liveness,
+no-alias and view-offset checks still apply. This exception is scoped to
+loop-carry propagation: accumulator if-phi coalescing still requires matching
+identity windows, because it derives the destination from the alias itself.
+The nested-phi regression checks that no redundant carry move is introduced;
+the divergent-accumulator-alias regression must continue to fail closed.
+
